<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:32:default:windows">
<!--
    x86-32 callee stack purge read off the callee's own returns (kuna_calleepurge).

    x86win.cspec leaves the extrapop of its __stdcall/__thiscall/__fastcall models
    unknown. A call no later merge pins gets StackSolver's guess, which for an
    internal callee is "pops nothing", so every stack offset after a __stdcall
    call is shifted by its argument bytes: later calls lose stack arguments and
    the caller's locals alias their outgoing-argument slots.

    Fixture: decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/stdcallpurge_pe_i386.exe
    (VC8 /O2, build notes in stdcallpurge_pe_i386.s). split (0x401080) calls the
    five-argument __stdcall dist twice and the six-argument __stdcall corte once,
    each through a `jmp` thunk, and passes &os/&ot to corte.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>clear architecture</com>
  <com>load file decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/stdcallpurge_pe_i386.exe</com>
  <com>read symbols</com>
  <com>function bounds 0x401080 as split</com>
  <com>load function split</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="CALLEEPURGE #1: the first thunked __stdcall call keeps all five arguments" min="1" max="1">v1 = \(float4\)\(float10\)sub_401190\(a0,a1,a2,a3,a4\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLEEPURGE #2: the second call is read at the stack depth the first one left" min="1" max="1">= \(float4\)\(float10\)sub_401190\(a1,a2,a3,a4,a0\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLEEPURGE #3: the six-argument call gets its arguments and the two local addresses" min="1" max="1">sub_401195\(v1,a4,a0,a1,&amp;v4,&amp;v3\)</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLEEPURGE #4: the locals the callee writes are read back, not split" min="1" max="1">return \(float10\)\(float4\)\(\(float10\)v4 \+ \(float10\)v3\);</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
